    Ransomware: Malware that encrypts files and demands payment for decryption
      ~Deploy ransomware through phishing emails containing malicious attachments (documents, PDFs) that download and execute the ransomware payload
      ~Exploit vulnerable public-facing services (RDP, VPN, web applications) to gain initial access and manually deploy ransomware
      ~Use compromised credentials from data breaches to access remote services (RDP, VPN) and deploy ransomware across network
      ~Deploy ransomware through malvertising campaigns that redirect to exploit kits that deliver the ransomware payload
      ~Use PowerShell or WMI to distribute ransomware laterally across network after gaining initial foothold
      ~Disable or corrupt system backups and shadow copies before encryption to prevent recovery
      ~Target specific file extensions and network shares for encryption while avoiding critical system files
      ~Create ransom notes in each encrypted directory with payment instructions and deadlines
      ~Exfiltrate sensitive data before encryption for double-extortion attacks
      ~Use legitimate tools (PsExec, Group Policy) to deploy ransomware across domain-joined systems
      ~Modify ransomware code to evade antivirus detection using packers, crypters, or custom obfuscation techniques
      ~Deploy ransomware through supply chain attacks by compromising software updates or third-party vendors
      ~Use living-off-the-land binaries (LOLBins) to download and execute ransomware payloads, evading detection
      ~Create persistence through scheduled tasks and registry keys to ensure ransomware executes after system reboots
      ~Deploy ransomware through compromised MSPs (Managed Service Providers) to target multiple organizations
      ~Use timestomping and file attribute manipulation to avoid detection by file monitoring systems
      ~Implement network scanning to identify and encrypt network-attached storage (NAS) devices
      ~Deploy ransomware through compromised remote monitoring and management (RMM) tools
      ~Use encrypted communication channels (TOR, custom C2) for ransom negotiations and payment processing
      ~Implement delayed execution techniques to bypass sandbox analysis and complicate incident response
      ~Target virtualization infrastructure (ESXi, vCenter) using specialized encryption routines to maximize impact
      ~Implement file-less ransomware execution using memory-only payloads and Windows API abuse
      ~Use kernel-level drivers (signed or exploited) to bypass security controls and ensure encryption success
      ~Target database servers with specific routines to corrupt or encrypt database files while in use
      ~Deploy ransomware through compromised Active Directory Certificate Services for signed malware delivery
      ~Implement boot-level encryption to prevent system startup and force ransom payment
      ~Use distributed computing techniques to speed up encryption of large enterprise environments
      ~Target cloud storage and backup solutions (AWS S3, Azure Blob) through compromised credentials
      ~Implement worm-like capabilities using multiple exploitation methods for self-propagation
      ~Deploy multiple ransomware variants simultaneously to complicate recovery efforts
      ~Target industrial control systems (ICS/SCADA) with specialized encryption routines that avoid system crashes
      ~Implement secure-erase procedures to prevent recovery through forensic analysis
      ~Use intermittent encryption to evade detection by behavioral analysis tools
      ~Deploy ransomware with time-bomb functionality triggered by specific dates or events
      ~Target backup software APIs directly to ensure destruction of backup catalogs and indexes
      ~Implement cryptocurrency wallet hijacking alongside encryption for additional revenue
      ~Use stolen code signing certificates from legitimate companies to sign ransomware binaries
      ~Deploy decoy ransomware to distract from actual data exfiltration activities
      ~Target disaster recovery sites simultaneously with production environments
      ~Implement geographic-based targeting to avoid specific countries or regions
      ~Implement partial encryption strategies to reduce detection while maintaining impact
      ~Deploy ransomware with built-in counter-incident response capabilities (detect and disable IR tools)
      ~Target specific industry-vertical applications (healthcare EMR, financial trading systems) for maximum leverage
      ~Use automated negotiation bots to handle victim communications and payment processing
      ~Implement cross-platform encryption capabilities (Windows, Linux, MacOS) in single deployment
      ~Create victim-specific ransom notes using exfiltrated company information for higher impact
      ~Deploy ransomware with selective targeting based on system/network profiling
      ~Implement dead-man switch functionality to auto-release data if payment timeline expires
      ~Use social media and press manipulation techniques to increase payment pressure
      ~Target source code repositories and development infrastructure to maximize intellectual property impact
