    Banking Trojans: Malware specifically targeting financial institutions and transactions
      ~Zeus/Zbot: Modular banking trojan with extensive stealing capabilities and bot functionality
      ~Emotet: Advanced banking trojan that evolved into a malware distribution platform
      ~TrickBot: Modular banking malware focusing on credential and financial data theft
      ~Dridex: Banking trojan targeting business accounts through malicious macros
      ~Gozi: Banking malware known for web injection and stolen credentials exfiltration
      ~Ursnif: Banking trojan specializing in form grabbing and information stealing
      ~Ramnit: Banking malware combining financial theft with worm-like spreading
      ~QakBot: Banking trojan with automated transfer system capabilities
      ~IcedID: Modern banking malware with stealer and backdoor functionality
      ~Neverquest/Vawtrak: Banking trojan focusing on account takeover and fraud
      ~Tinba: One of the smallest banking trojans, known for its compact size and stealth
      ~Shifu: Japanese-focused banking trojan combining features from Zeus and Dridex
      ~Danabot: Modular banking malware targeting Australian and European financial institutions
      ~GozNym: Hybrid malware combining Gozi ISFB and Nymaim components
      ~Kronos: Banking trojan marketed in underground forums with Zeus-like features
      ~Retefe: Swiss and Swedish focused banking trojan using proxy redirection
      ~Sphinx: Modified Zeus variant targeting mobile banking applications
      ~Carbanak: Advanced banking malware used in targeted bank attacks
      ~Carberp: Russian banking trojan with rootkit capabilities
      ~Shylock: Banking trojan targeting UK financial institutions
      ~Bugat/Feodo: Banking trojan known for frequent code updates and resilience
      ~Caphaw/Shylock: Sophisticated banking malware with advanced evasion
      ~Citadel: Zeus-based banking trojan with ransomware capabilities
      ~Dyre/Dyreza: Banking malware targeting corporate banking credentials
      ~Gootkit: Banking trojan with advanced browser manipulation features
      ~IcedID: Modular banking malware with backup C2 mechanisms
      ~Marcher: Android-based banking trojan targeting mobile applications
      ~Panda Banker: Modern modular banking trojan derived from Zeus
      ~Spyeye: Historic competitor to Zeus with account takeover capabilities
      ~Zloader: Modern banking trojan based on Zeus source code
      ~BackSwap: Banking trojan specialized in avoiding browser injection detection
      ~Metamorfo: Brazilian banking trojan with screenshotting capabilities
      ~Silent Night: Modern Zeus-based trojan sold as malware-as-a-service
      ~Geodo: Evolution of Emotet with specialized banking features
      ~Dreambot: Advanced variant of Ursnif with extended stealing capabilities
      ~FlawedAmmyy: Banking trojan based on leaked Ammyy Admin source code
      ~Bizzaro: South American banking trojan targeting cloud services
      ~TeaBot: Android banking trojan targeting European financial institutions
      ~Gustuff: Automated Android banking trojan with ATS features
      ~IAmTheKing: Russian banking trojan with USB spreading capabilities
    Cryptominers: Malware that hijacks system resources for cryptocurrency mining
      ~Deploy XMRig miner through malicious scripts, configuring it to mine Monero with attacker's wallet address and pool settings
      ~Install hidden mining service that masquerades as legitimate system process, using process hollowing to evade detection
      ~Implement CPU throttling and monitoring suspension to avoid detection through system performance degradation
      ~Create persistence through scheduled tasks and registry keys, with backup mechanisms if primary method is discovered
      ~Deploy network-spreading components to infect additional systems using exploits or stolen credentials
      ~Configure mining operations to only run during low-activity hours using system monitoring triggers
      ~Implement watchdog processes to restart mining operations if terminated by security tools
      ~Use DNS tunneling for pool communication to bypass network security monitoring
      ~Deploy rootkit components to hide mining processes and network connections from system tools
      ~Implement cryptocurrency wallet address rotation to avoid tracking of mining proceeds
      ~Inject mining code into compromised web applications to perform in-browser mining using visitors' resources
      ~Deploy GPU-optimized miners for Ethereum/other GPU-friendly coins with custom drivers to bypass monitoring
      ~Implement peer-to-peer communication between infected hosts to share mining pools and updated configurations
      ~Use fileless execution techniques to load miners directly into memory from encrypted network sources
      ~Deploy decoy mining processes to distract from actual mining operations while monitoring for analysis attempts
      ~Implement geofencing to avoid mining in specific countries or IP ranges that might trigger investigation
      ~Use code signing and fake certificates to make mining executables appear legitimate
      ~Deploy mining code through supply chain compromise in legitimate software packages
      ~Implement sandbox detection and delayed execution to avoid detection during malware analysis
      ~Use kernel-level drivers to intercept and modify system monitoring tools' results
      ~Implement dynamic algorithm switching based on hardware capabilities and cryptocurrency profitability
      ~Deploy container-based mining operations that isolate mining processes from host system detection
      ~Use living-off-the-land techniques to leverage legitimate mining software with malicious configurations
      ~Implement cross-platform compilation to target Windows, Linux, and macOS systems simultaneously
      ~Deploy mining operations through compromised cloud service credentials using stolen compute resources
      ~Implement failover mechanisms to switch between multiple mining pools if primary connections are blocked
      ~Use timestomping and binary padding to make mining executables appear as legitimate system files
      ~Deploy network proxies to route mining traffic through compromised IoT devices
      ~Implement system restore point deletion to prevent recovery and miner removal
      ~Use code polymorphism to generate unique mining executables for each target
      ~Implement automatic cryptocurrency tumbling/mixing to obscure mining proceeds origin
      ~Deploy fake error messages and system notifications to explain away performance issues to users
      ~Use UEFI/BIOS level persistence to survive operating system reinstallation
      ~Implement distributed mining pool creation to avoid detection through pool traffic analysis
      ~Deploy counter-forensics tools to remove mining operation logs and traces
      ~Use system resource quota management to distribute mining load across CPU cores stealthily
      ~Implement automated backup command & control infrastructure for mining pool coordination
      ~Deploy network traffic morphing to make mining communications appear as legitimate protocols
      ~Use hardware-level optimization through microcode manipulation for improved mining efficiency
      ~Implement self-destruct mechanisms if forensic tools or specific security products are detected
    Worms: Self-propagating malware that spreads across networks
      ~Exploit SMB vulnerabilities (like EternalBlue) to propagate across Windows networks by scanning for and attacking vulnerable SMB services on port 445
      ~Use brute force attacks against weak passwords on network shares and administrative accounts to spread laterally
      ~Deploy malicious payloads through network shares that auto-execute using Windows AutoRun functionality
      ~Exploit email client vulnerabilities to self-propagate by sending malicious messages to contacts in address books
      ~Scan for and exploit known vulnerabilities in web servers and applications to spread across internet-facing systems
      ~Create backdoor accounts and modify security settings to maintain access on infected systems
      ~Use USB drives and removable media for propagation by infecting autorun.inf and creating hidden malicious executables
      ~Exploit zero-day vulnerabilities in network protocols and services for stealth propagation
      ~Implement domain credential harvesting to facilitate spread across Active Directory environments
      ~Use built-in Windows tools (WMI, PowerShell) for lateral movement while evading detection
      ~Exploit RDP vulnerabilities and weak configurations for propagation, using techniques like BlueKeep (CVE-2019-0708)
      ~Implement polymorphic code to modify malware signature during propagation and evade antivirus detection
      ~Target industrial control systems by exploiting SCADA protocols and vulnerable PLCs
      ~Use IRC channels for command and control while spreading through IRC networks and clients
      ~Exploit printer spooler vulnerabilities (like PrintNightmare) for lateral movement across enterprise networks
      ~Implement network scanning with randomized patterns and delays to avoid detection
      ~Target cloud storage sync clients to spread through shared folders and cloud storage services
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in peer-to-peer file sharing networks and clients
      ~Use DNS tunneling for command and control while propagating through internal networks
      ~Target collaboration software (like messaging apps) to spread through enterprise chat systems
      ~Target container environments by exploiting vulnerabilities in container runtimes and orchestration platforms
      ~Exploit IoT device vulnerabilities and default credentials for propagation across smart device networks
      ~Use DNS rebinding attacks to compromise internal networks through exposed IoT devices
      ~Implement multi-stage propagation using dropper modules that download additional payloads
      ~Target software update mechanisms and package managers for supply chain based propagation
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in VPN appliances and remote access gateways
      ~Use IPv6 tunneling and transition mechanisms to bypass network security controls
      ~Target network backup systems and disaster recovery infrastructure for propagation
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in network monitoring and management tools
      ~Use cross-platform compilation to target multiple operating systems and architectures simultaneously
      ~Target software-defined networking (SDN) controllers and network virtualization layers
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in serverless function platforms and cloud service APIs
      ~Propagate through wireless protocols like Bluetooth and Zigbee in IoT networks
      ~Target network time synchronization protocols (NTP) for internal network propagation
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in network access control (NAC) systems to bypass segmentation
      ~Use compromised mobile devices to spread through enterprise MDM systems
      ~Target building automation systems and smart building infrastructure
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in healthcare protocols and medical devices (DICOM, HL7)
      ~Use compromised CI/CD pipelines for propagation through development environments
      ~Target network storage protocols (iSCSI, NFS) and storage area networks (SANs)
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in 5G network infrastructure and network slicing components
      ~Target quantum key distribution (QKD) systems and post-quantum cryptography implementations
      ~Exploit hypervisor escape vulnerabilities for cross-VM propagation
      ~Target financial messaging systems and payment networks (SWIFT, ISO 20022)
      ~Exploit SS7 and Diameter protocols in telecommunications networks
      ~Target blockchain nodes and distributed ledger infrastructure
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in edge computing nodes and fog computing infrastructure
      ~Target satellite communication systems and ground station infrastructure
      ~Exploit vulnerabilities in network function virtualization (NFV) components
      ~Target high-performance computing (HPC) clusters and grid computing systems
    Keyloggers: Malware designed to capture keystrokes and user input
      ~Perfect Keylogger - Commercial keylogger with advanced features including keystroke recording, screenshots, and remote access
      ~KidLogger - Monitoring software often abused for malicious purposes
