    Mobile Malware: Malware specifically designed for mobile platforms
      ~Deploy mobile malware through compromised app stores or third-party marketplaces by repackaging legitimate apps with malicious code and using similar app names/icons to deceive users
      ~Exploit mobile browser vulnerabilities through malvertising campaigns and drive-by downloads to deliver malware payload
      ~Abuse Android accessibility services to gain persistent access, intercept user interactions, and harvest credentials
      ~Implement SMS/call interception capabilities to steal MFA codes and conduct financial fraud
      ~Establish persistence through system app privileges, boot receivers, and scheduled tasks
      ~Harvest sensitive data including contacts, messages, photos, and stored credentials from mobile device storage
      ~Monitor device location and user activity through GPS tracking and keylogging functionality
      ~Abuse mobile device management (MDM) profiles to maintain control and hide malicious activities
      ~Implement command and control through cloud messaging services (FCM/APNs) to evade detection
      ~Extract authentication tokens and session cookies from mobile browsers and apps for account takeover
      ~Implement root/jailbreak detection bypass to hide from mobile security solutions and banking apps
      ~Abuse enterprise certificate signing to deploy malware on iOS devices through sideloading
      ~Exploit mobile baseband vulnerabilities to intercept cellular communications and perform MITM attacks
      ~Deploy overlay attacks targeting mobile banking apps to steal financial credentials and conduct fraudulent transactions
      ~Extract enterprise credentials and VPN configurations to facilitate lateral movement into corporate networks
      ~Abuse mobile device backup/restore functions to maintain persistence across device resets
      ~Implement screen recording and remote viewing capabilities through WebRTC or native APIs
      ~Deploy mobile ransomware that encrypts device storage and demands payment through mobile payment systems
      ~Exploit mobile app deep links and URL schemes for command execution and data theft
      ~Implement cross-platform payload delivery using progressive web apps and mobile browser exploits
      ~Exploit mobile device sensors (microphone, camera, accelerometer) for environmental surveillance and user behavior profiling
      ~Deploy fake base stations (IMSI catchers) to intercept mobile communications and force malware delivery
      ~Implement mobile cryptojacking through WebAssembly and native code to mine cryptocurrency
      ~Compromise mobile app development supply chain by injecting malicious SDKs and libraries
      ~Exploit NFC and Bluetooth vulnerabilities to spread malware to nearby devices and IoT equipment
      ~Deploy social engineering tactics through messaging apps and SMS to spread malware using contact lists
      ~Implement mobile botnet capabilities for DDoS attacks and spam distribution
      ~Exploit mobile payment and NFC payment systems for financial fraud
      ~Abuse mobile device backup services (iCloud, Google backup) for data exfiltration and persistence
      ~Target mobile device firmware and bootloader for persistent rootkit installation
    Fileless Malware: Malware that operates primarily in memory without writing to disk
      ~PowerShell Empire: Post-exploitation framework that operates entirely through PowerShell scripts in memory
      ~Meterpreter: Memory-resident payload that provides an interactive shell without touching disk
      ~Kovter: Fileless malware that persists through registry keys and PowerShell
      ~HiddenWasp: Linux-based fileless malware that maintains persistence in memory
      ~Astaroth: Fileless trojan that leverages native Windows processes
      ~Frodo: Fileless backdoor that uses legitimate Windows processes for execution
