      ~Nodersok: Fileless malware that abuses legitimate tools like Node.js
      ~Divergent: Fileless malware that uses regsvr32.exe for execution
      ~Poweliks: Registry-based fileless malware that injects code into legitimate processes
      ~RogueRobin: Fileless malware that uses DNS tunneling for C2 communication
      ~FritzFrog: P2P fileless botnet that operates in memory
      ~Metamorfo: Banking trojan that operates filelessly using legitimate Windows tools
      ~PinkSlipBot: Fileless banking malware with worm capabilities
      ~Mosquito: Fileless backdoor using custom TCP protocol
      ~Ramsay: Air-gapped targeting malware with fileless components
      ~Turtle: Fileless crypto-mining malware using WMI
      ~LemonDuck: Fileless crypto-mining botnet targeting both Windows and Linux
      ~VaporRage: Memory-only ransomware using direct syscalls
      ~CactusTorch: .NET-based fileless malware leveraging DotNetToJScript
      ~RubyMiner: Fileless cryptocurrency miner targeting Linux servers
      ~Phasebot: Fileless modular banking trojan using reflective DLL injection
      ~GUDWIN: Memory-resident malware using COM hijacking
      ~Mailto/NetWalker: Fileless ransomware using reflective loading
      ~Waterbear: Fileless backdoor with multi-stage loading process
      ~JSWorm: Fileless ransomware using JavaScript and WScript
      ~Lucifer: Hybrid cryptojacking malware with fileless components
      ~PoshC2: Framework for generating fileless payloads
      ~Sorebrect: Fileless ransomware targeting enterprise systems
      ~RogueOne: Fileless spyware using Windows Management Instrumentation
      ~HolesWarm: Fileless worm targeting multiple vulnerabilities in Linux
      ~Jupyter: Fileless .NET infostealer using PowerShell
      ~BlackSquid: Fileless cryptominer using multiple exploitation tools
      ~Bandook: Fileless RAT with complex obfuscation techniques
      ~ChessMaster: Fileless backdoor using alternative data streams
      ~Helminth: Fileless rootkit using direct kernel manipulation
      ~Golang-based Fileless Rat: Pure memory resident RAT written in Go
      ~Attor: Fileless modular espionage platform
      ~InvisiMole: Sophisticated fileless spyware using multiple modules
      ~Turla's Carbon: Fileless second-stage malware framework
    Wipers: Malware designed to destroy data and systems
      ~Deploy wiper malware via compromised software updates, targeting system files and boot records to render systems inoperable
      ~Use scheduled tasks to trigger simultaneous data wiping across network segments at predetermined times
      ~Target specific file extensions (.doc, .pdf, .db) for selective data destruction while maintaining system operation
      ~Overwrite Master Boot Record (MBR) with malicious code to prevent system boot and recovery
      ~Implement recursive file deletion and overwriting across mapped network drives and shared folders
      ~Disable system recovery tools and volume shadow copies before initiating wiping procedures
      ~Encrypt files with randomly generated keys before deletion to prevent forensic recovery
      ~Target critical system services and processes to cause cascading system failures
      ~Use kernel-level drivers to bypass security controls and directly access storage devices
      ~Implement self-propagation mechanisms to spread wiper functionality across networked systems
      ~Exploit Active Directory infrastructure to deploy wiper payloads through group policy objects (GPOs)
      ~Target database management systems by corrupting database files and transaction logs
      ~Deploy wipers through compromised remote management tools and IT administration software
      ~Implement time-delayed activation to coordinate widespread destruction across multiple targets
      ~Target specific industrial control system (ICS) components and configurations for destruction
      ~Use legitimate file shredding algorithms to ensure secure deletion of targeted data
      ~Target backup systems and archival storage before initiating main wiping sequence
      ~Exploit firmware update mechanisms to deploy low-level system destruction capabilities
      ~Target cloud storage synchronization to propagate destruction to connected systems
      ~Implement network segmentation evasion to reach air-gapped systems
      ~Exploit printer and network device firmware for cross-platform wiper deployment
      ~Target virtualization infrastructure by corrupting VM disk images and hypervisor configurations
      ~Use living-off-the-land techniques to blend wiper activities with legitimate system operations
      ~Implement fileless wiping techniques using memory-resident components
      ~Target source code repositories and development infrastructure for intellectual property destruction
      ~Deploy wipers through compromised software supply chain components
      ~Target specific cryptocurrency wallets and blockchain nodes for financial disruption
      ~Implement wiper functionality that mimics ransomware to mislead incident response efforts
      ~Target disaster recovery sites and continuity systems to prevent business restoration
      ~Exploit mobile device management (MDM) systems to deploy wipers to endpoint devices
      ~Target DNS infrastructure to disrupt name resolution before initiating wiping sequence
      ~Exploit identity and access management systems to gain elevated destruction privileges
      ~Target software license servers to trigger widespread application failures
      ~Implement geographic-based triggering to coordinate destruction across multiple time zones
      ~Target storage area network (SAN) configurations and management interfaces
      ~Deploy wipers through compromised security tools and EDR systems
      ~Target certificate authorities and PKI infrastructure to disrupt authentication systems
      ~Exploit network monitoring tools to hide destruction activities and disable alerts
      ~Target configuration management databases (CMDB) to disrupt IT service management
      ~Implement selective wiping based on system language and regional settings
