    Browser Hijackers: Malware that modifies browser settings and behavior
      ~Modify browser homepage, search engine, and new tab settings through registry manipulation (HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run)
      ~Install malicious browser extensions by injecting code into browser extension directories and modifying extension manifests
      ~Intercept and modify browser traffic by installing root certificates and configuring proxy settings
      ~Deploy through bundled software installers using deceptive opt-out options and hidden components
      ~Establish persistence through browser helper objects (BHOs) and startup registry keys
      ~Inject JavaScript code into browsed pages to modify content and insert unwanted advertisements
      ~Harvest browser data including cookies, saved passwords, and browsing history
      ~Create rogue DNS entries to redirect web traffic to malicious sites
      ~Disable browser security features and updates through policy modifications
      ~Monitor and intercept browser API calls to capture user input and session data
      ~Replace legitimate DLL files with malicious versions to intercept browser function calls
      ~Create scheduled tasks to periodically check and restore malicious browser configurations
      ~Implement browser process injection using CreateRemoteThread for stealth operation
      ~Deploy filter drivers to intercept and modify browser network traffic at the kernel level
      ~Modify browser preference files directly (e.g., prefs.js for Firefox, Preferences for Chrome)
      ~Install malicious browser protocol handlers to hijack URL scheme handling
      ~Deploy browser helper objects (BHOs) through COM object registration
      ~Implement anti-removal mechanisms by monitoring and restoring hijacked settings
      ~Create shadow copies of malicious components in multiple system locations
      ~Modify browser security zones and trusted site configurations
      ~Implement browser process hollowing to hide malicious code in legitimate browser processes
      ~Modify browser certificate stores to enable SSL/TLS interception
      ~Deploy browser-specific shellcode through exploit techniques targeting rendering engines
      ~Create malicious service workers for offline operation and persistent control
      ~Implement cross-browser infection by targeting shared components and plugins
      ~Deploy man-in-the-browser (MitB) attacks through browser API hooking
      ~Modify browser sandbox configurations to bypass security restrictions
      ~Implement browser memory manipulation to inject malicious code into protected pages
      ~Deploy browser-specific rootkit techniques to hide malicious activities
      ~Create synchronized profiles across browsers to maintain persistent control
      ~Exploit browser password managers to extract and modify stored credentials
      ~Implement browser fingerprinting to track users across sessions and domains
      ~Deploy malicious browser automation scripts for automated fraud
      ~Modify browser WebRTC configurations to leak real IP addresses
      ~Implement browser-based cryptocurrency miners through injected scripts
      ~Create malicious Progressive Web Apps (PWAs) for persistent access
      ~Deploy browser-based keyloggers through input event manipulation
      ~Modify browser MIME handlers to execute malicious content
      ~Implement cross-origin resource hijacking through browser policy manipulation
      ~Create browser-based backdoors through custom protocol handlers
      ~Exploit browser extension APIs to access privileged functions
      ~Implement browser-based form grabbers through DOM manipulation
      ~Deploy malicious browser serviceworker interceptors
      ~Modify browser IndexedDB/WebSQL databases to persist malicious data
      ~Implement browser cache poisoning through service worker manipulation
      ~Create malicious browser push notification subscriptions
      ~Deploy browser-based screen capture through MediaDevices API abuse
      ~Modify browser WebAssembly modules for stealth operation
      ~Implement browser storage quota exploitation for denial of service
      ~Create browser-based side-channel attacks through timing analysis
    Industrial Control System (ICS) Malware: Malware targeting industrial systems and SCADA
      ~TRITON/TRISIS: Deploy malware targeting Triconex Safety Instrumented System (SIS) controllers, modify safety logic, and potentially cause physical damage
      ~Industroyer/Crashoverride: Target IEC-60870-5-101, IEC-60870-5-104, IEC 61850, and OPC DA protocols to manipulate circuit breakers and cause power outages
      ~Stuxnet: Target Siemens S7 PLCs through infected Step 7 software, modify centrifuge operations while hiding changes from operators
      ~BlackEnergy: Compromise HMI software using stolen certificates, establish persistence, and enable remote control of ICS systems
      ~Havex: Compromise ICS vendor websites, distribute trojanized software updates, scan OPC servers, and collect ICS network information
      ~PLC-Blaster: Infect Siemens S7 PLCs, spread through network scanning, and establish backdoor access
      ~EKANS/Snake: Deploy ransomware specifically targeting ICS environments, terminate ICS processes, and encrypt system files
      ~Rogue7: Target Siemens S7-1200/1500 PLCs, manipulate control logic, and maintain persistence
      ~Kill Disk: Wipe master boot records and critical system files in ICS environments
      ~Irongate: Target Siemens PLCs by intercepting communications between PLCs and HMI systems, inject false process data
      ~Pipedream/INCONTROLLER: Target Schneider Electric and OMRON controllers, interact with specific industrial equipment, modify PLC logic
      ~WannaCry ICS Variant: Target vulnerable ICS systems running Windows, exploit SMB vulnerabilities, encrypt industrial data and configurations
      ~NotPetya ICS Impact: Spread through MEDoc software update, target industrial networks, disable industrial systems through disk encryption
      ~Duqu 2.0: Perform reconnaissance in ICS environments, gather intelligence about industrial processes, maintain long-term access
      ~Shamoon ICS: Target energy sector networks, wipe industrial systems, destroy operational data
      ~COPPERFIELD: Target building automation systems (BAS), manipulate HVAC controls, collect facility data
      ~Industroyer2: Enhanced version targeting multiple IEC protocols, specifically designed for Ukraine's power grid
      ~Backdoor.Oldrea: Perform targeted espionage in energy sector, collect ICS configuration data, establish persistent access
      ~Sandworm ICS Tools: Target GE Proficy HMI/SCADA systems, enable remote manipulation of industrial processes
      ~Chrysene: Target oil and gas SCADA systems, establish persistence, enable remote control
      ~HatMan/ICSSYSTEM: Target Schneider Electric Triconex controllers, modify memory of safety controllers, disable safety systems
      ~XENOTIME Tools: Target safety instrumented systems (SIS) across multiple vendors, enable process manipulation
      ~PLC Wiper: Erase PLC logic and firmware, render industrial equipment inoperable
      ~Water Treatment Malware: Target chemical dosing systems in water treatment facilities, modify HMI displays
      ~Covellite: Target power grid operations, establish persistence in energy sector networks
      ~ELECTRICFISH: Tunnel traffic between ICS networks, enable remote access to isolated systems
      ~FlowCalc: Target flow computers in oil and gas sector, manipulate measurement data
      ~MEMZ ICS: Target manufacturing execution systems (MES), disrupt production processes
      ~PyInstaller Loader: Package ICS-specific malicious Python scripts, target engineering workstations
      ~Industroyer.V2: Target multiple vendor PLCs, specifically designed for power distribution systems
      ~ModifiedCNA: Target Modbus protocol communications, intercept and modify control commands
      ~LookBack: Target utilities sector, create backdoors in grid control systems
      ~ZeroCleare: Wipe industrial systems in energy sector, specifically target Middle Eastern facilities
      ~Siprotec Attack Tool: Target Siemens Siprotec protection relays, disable protective functions
      ~CrashOverride.V2: Enhanced version targeting substation automation protocols
      ~SCADA Shutdown Tool: Force emergency shutdowns in industrial facilities
      ~MegaLocker ICS: Target historians and data acquisition systems, encrypt industrial databases
      ~BadEnergy: Target smart meter infrastructure, manipulate energy consumption data
      ~GreyEnergy: Target industrial network protocols, establish long-term reconnaissance
      ~PLC RootKit: Hide malicious logic in multiple PLC brands while maintaining normal operation displays
