    API Hooking Malware: Malware that intercepts and modifies API calls
      ~Implement inline API hooking by modifying function prologue with JMP instruction to redirect to malicious code
      ~Use Import Address Table (IAT) hooking to intercept API calls by modifying function pointers
      ~Deploy Export Address Table (EAT) hooking to redirect API calls at the DLL export level
      ~Install system-wide hooks using SetWindowsHookEx for keyboard and mouse monitoring
      ~Implement DLL injection combined with API hooking to intercept process communications
      ~Hook security-related APIs (CreateFile, RegOpenKey) to bypass security controls
      ~Intercept network APIs (send, recv) for data theft and communication monitoring
      ~Hook memory management APIs (VirtualAlloc, HeapAlloc) to hide malicious allocations
      ~Implement userland rootkit functionality through extensive API hooking
      ~Hook process creation APIs (CreateProcess) to maintain persistence and monitor new processes
      ~Hook cryptographic APIs (CryptoAPI) to intercept encryption/decryption operations and steal keys
      ~Implement SSDT (System Service Descriptor Table) hooking for kernel-level API interception
      ~Hook file system minifilter APIs to intercept and modify file operations
      ~Deploy message hooking for intercepting inter-process communication (IPC)
      ~Hook authentication APIs to capture credentials and tokens
      ~Implement COM/RPC API hooking to intercept component object model communications
      ~Hook debugging APIs to evade detection and analysis
      ~Implement TLS callback hooking for early execution and API interception
      ~Hook DNS resolution APIs to manipulate network connectivity
      ~Deploy thread execution hijacking through API hooks in thread creation functions
      ~Hook graphics/UI APIs (GDI, DirectX) for screen capture and keylogging
      ~Implement hooking of Anti-Virus APIs to bypass security scanning
      ~Hook power management APIs to maintain persistence across system sleep states
      ~Deploy clipboard API hooks to intercept copied data
      ~Implement USB/HID device API hooks for hardware access control
      ~Hook event logging APIs to suppress malware activity traces
      ~Implement browser API hooks to intercept web traffic and credentials
      ~Hook memory scanning APIs to prevent memory analysis
      ~Implement printer spooler API hooks for document interception
      ~Hook Windows notification APIs to monitor system events
      ~Hook Windows Update APIs to prevent security patches
      ~Implement shell extension API hooks for filesystem operation monitoring
      ~Hook remote desktop APIs to intercept RDP sessions
      ~Deploy audio API hooks for room monitoring through microphone
      ~Implement Windows Search APIs hooks to monitor file indexing
      ~Hook certificate validation APIs to bypass SSL/TLS verification
      ~Implement WMI provider API hooks for system information gathering
      ~Hook backup APIs to prevent system restore capabilities
      ~Implement network adapter APIs hooks for network traffic manipulation
      ~Hook Windows Security Center APIs to disable security notifications
      ~Hook Windows Defender APIs to bypass real-time protection
      ~Implement AppContainer/Isolation APIs hooks for sandbox escape
      ~Hook Windows Hello biometric APIs to intercept authentication data
      ~Deploy Windows Firewall APIs hooks to create hidden network rules
      ~Implement DRM protection APIs hooks to bypass content restrictions
      ~Hook Windows Service Manager APIs for service manipulation
      ~Implement Group Policy APIs hooks to bypass security policies
      ~Hook Windows Task Scheduler APIs for hidden task creation
      ~Implement Virtual Machine Detection APIs hooks to evade analysis
      ~Hook Windows Error Reporting APIs to suppress crash reports
    VM/Sandbox Evasion Malware: Malware specifically designed to detect and evade analysis environments
      ~Check for VM artifacts by scanning registry keys, processes, and files associated with common virtualization platforms (VMware, VirtualBox, QEMU)
      ~Detect sandbox environments by checking memory size, disk space, and number of processors against minimum thresholds
      ~Monitor user interaction patterns (mouse movements, keyboard input) to identify automated analysis systems
      ~Use timing-based detection by measuring execution delays between instructions or API calls
      ~Scan for debugging tools, monitoring processes, and analysis frameworks
      ~Check for network artifacts associated with common sandbox environments
      ~Verify presence of common applications and browser history to detect artificial environments
      ~Use CPU-specific instructions or features that may not be properly emulated
      ~Implement sleep timers and delayed execution to outlast sandbox analysis timeouts
      ~Query hardware identifiers and system characteristics for signs of virtualization
      ~Check for common sandbox hostnames, usernames, and domain names used in analysis environments
      ~Detect screen resolution and monitor count to identify virtual environments
      ~Implement anti-debugging techniques (IsDebuggerPresent, CheckRemoteDebuggerPresent API calls)
      ~Monitor temperature and power status changes to detect hardware simulation
      ~Check for specific drivers and device IDs commonly used in virtual environments
      ~Verify network card MAC addresses against known VM vendor ranges
      ~Test for memory artifacts created by dynamic analysis tools
      ~Detect system uptime and installation dates to identify fresh analysis environments
      ~Monitor for specific registry keys created by automated analysis tools
      ~Check for language settings and regional configurations common in analysis labs
      ~Implement code integrity checks to detect dynamic analysis modifications
      ~Use TLS callback functions to execute code before main program entry
      ~Check for common sandbox communication patterns (DNS requests, HTTP proxies)
      ~Detect presence of common sandbox DLL injection techniques
      ~Monitor disk performance metrics to identify virtual storage systems
      ~Check for USB device presence and consistency typical in real systems
      ~Verify system font collections against those commonly found in analysis environments
      ~Monitor process creation patterns typical of analysis environments
      ~Check for artifacts of common memory dumping tools
      ~Implement branch timing attacks to detect instruction-level analysis
      ~Detect cloud-based sandbox environments through network latency analysis
      ~Check for hardware-assisted virtualization features and hypervisor presence
      ~Implement WMI queries to detect analysis tool artifacts
      ~Monitor for system restore points and snapshot indicators
      ~Check for audio device presence and functionality
      ~Detect common sandbox memory patterns and structures
      ~Verify GPU capabilities and DirectX features
      ~Monitor for automated input simulation tools
      ~Check for virtualized network adapter configurations
      ~Detect sandbox-specific API hooks and patches
      ~Check BIOS/UEFI characteristics and firmware tables
      ~Monitor power management and ACPI behavior patterns
      ~Detect kernel-level debugging and analysis tools
      ~Verify system service patterns and dependencies
      ~Check for side-channel analysis tools and monitoring
      ~Detect hardware performance counter manipulation
      ~Monitor DMA (Direct Memory Access) behavior patterns
      ~Check for virtual TPM (Trusted Platform Module) implementations
      ~Detect sandbox-specific network stack modifications
      ~Verify system interrupt handling patterns
    Bootkit Malware: Malware that infects the master boot record or volume boot record
      ~Install bootkit by overwriting MBR with malicious code that loads before OS boot:
      ~- Inject custom bootloader code into MBR sectors
      ~- Modify boot configuration data (BCD)
      ~- Chain load original MBR to maintain system functionality
      ~- Hide malicious code in unused disk sectors
      ~Establish persistence through VBR modification:
      ~- Modify volume boot record
      ~- Hook disk I/O operations
      ~- Intercept boot process
      ~- Install hidden storage areas for malware components
      ~Implement kernel-mode rootkit functionality:
      ~- Hook system calls
      ~- Hide malware processes and files
      ~- Intercept network traffic
