    Polymorphic Malware: Self-modifying malware that changes its code signature
      ~Implement code mutation using metamorphic engines that randomly change instruction sequences while maintaining functionality
      ~Deploy runtime encryption/decryption routines that dynamically decrypt code segments before execution
      ~Utilize oligomorphic engines to apply predefined sets of encryption algorithms with different encryption keys
      ~Implement garbage code insertion and dead code removal to modify binary signature
      ~Use code transposition techniques to randomly reorder independent code blocks
      ~Apply register reassignment to modify register usage patterns while preserving functionality
      ~Implement instruction substitution by replacing instructions with equivalent alternatives
      ~Deploy multi-layer packing with random packers to obfuscate the final payload
      ~Use polymorphic shellcode generators to create unique exploit payloads
      ~Implement memory-resident polymorphism that modifies code during runtime execution
      ~Implement environmental keying to modify code based on system-specific parameters
      ~Deploy anti-emulation techniques by detecting sandbox environments and modifying behavior
      ~Use timing-based polymorphism to change code patterns based on temporal triggers
      ~Implement API hooking with polymorphic hooks that change signatures periodically
      ~Deploy polymorphic buffer overflow techniques with randomized payload structures
      ~Use polymorphic XOR encoding with rotating keys for data and code segments
      ~Implement code splitting and reassembly across multiple files or memory locations
      ~Deploy network-aware polymorphism that changes behavior based on network context
      ~Use polymorphic process injection techniques with randomized injection patterns
      ~Implement metamorphic engine obfuscation to hide the mutation engine itself
      ~Implement polymorphic file infection techniques that modify host files differently each time
      ~Deploy self-modifying code segments that react to system patches and updates
      ~Use polymorphic memory residency techniques with dynamic memory layout changes
      ~Implement cross-platform polymorphic engines that adapt to different operating systems
