    DNS Hijacking Malware: Malware that manipulates DNS settings and queries
      ~Modify system DNS settings through registry manipulation (e.g., HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\Tcpip\Parameters) to redirect traffic to malicious DNS servers
      ~Install malicious browser extensions or plugins that override default DNS settings and intercept DNS queries
      ~Deploy rogue DHCP server to distribute malicious DNS configurations to network clients
      ~Inject malicious code into DNS client service (dnscache) to intercept and modify DNS queries in memory
      ~Create persistent DNS tunneling channel for C2 communication and data exfiltration using DNS TXT records
      ~Implement local DNS response spoofing by modifying hosts file and DNS resolver cache
      ~Deploy man-in-the-middle proxy to intercept and modify DNS traffic at network level
      ~Install rootkit components to hide malicious DNS configurations from system tools and security software
      ~Configure scheduled tasks to periodically update malicious DNS settings and maintain persistence
      ~Implement domain generation algorithm (DGA) for dynamic C2 domain resolution and evasion
      ~Exploit DNS cache poisoning by flooding DNS resolver with spoofed responses
      ~Modify DNS zone files on compromised DNS servers to redirect entire domains
      ~Deploy DNS rebinding attacks to bypass same-origin policy and access internal networks
      ~Implement fast-flux DNS techniques to rotate through multiple IP addresses for resilient C2
      ~Install network driver filter to intercept and modify DNS packets at kernel level
      ~Create malicious DNS responder service to selectively respond to specific DNS queries
      ~Deploy split DNS attack to serve different responses based on query source
      ~Implement DNS amplification capability for potential DDoS attacks
      ~Create covert channel using DNS NXDOMAIN responses for stealthy data transfer
      ~Modify PAC (Proxy Auto-Config) files to redirect DNS through malicious proxy servers
      ~Exploit DNSSEC validation vulnerabilities to bypass security controls
      ~Implement DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) proxying to evade traditional DNS monitoring
      ~Deploy malicious DNS64/NAT64 server for IPv6 network manipulation
      ~Create backdoor by modifying Active Directory-integrated DNS zones
      ~Implement DNS timing attack to perform stealthy data exfiltration
      ~Deploy malicious DNS forwarder to intercept and modify recursive queries
      ~Exploit dynamic DNS update vulnerabilities for zone poisoning
      ~Implement NSEC/NSEC3 walking technique to enumerate internal domains
      ~Create malicious DNS resolver policy for selective query manipulation
